The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
for (i = 0; i < BQ25980_NUM_WD_VAL; i++) {
    if (bq->watchdog_timer > bq25980_watchdog_time[i] &&
        bq->watchdog_timer < bq25980_watchdog_time[i + 1]) {
        wd_reg_val = i;
        break;
    }
}
```
Reason:
The loop iterates through the `bq25980_watchdog_time` array without checking if `i` is at the last index. When `i` equals `BQ25980_NUM_WD_VAL - 1`, the code attempts to access `bq25980_watchdog_time[i + 1]`, which is out of bounds, leading to undefined behavior or crashes.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
int wd_max_val = BQ25980_NUM_WD_VAL - 1;
if (bq->watchdog_timer >= bq25980_watchdog_time[wd_max_val])
    wd_reg_val = wd_max_val;
else {
    for (i = 0; i < wd_max_val; i++) {
        if (bq->watchdog_timer > bq25980_watchdog_time[i] &&
            bq->watchdog_timer < bq25980_watchdog_time[i + 1]) {
            wd_reg_val = i;
            break;
        }
    }
}
```
Reason:
The fix introduces a check to see if `bq->watchdog_timer` is greater than or equal to the last element of the `bq25980_watchdog_time` array. If it is, `wd_reg_val` is set to the maximum valid index, preventing any out-of-bounds access. The loop is then modified to iterate only up to `wd_max_val`, ensuring that the access to `bq25980_watchdog_time[i + 1]` is always valid.

